  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realize a task you own may miss its deadline. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise the risk early, explain the cause, and propose a recovery plan$qb$, $qb$Tell the person waiting for it that there may be a delay$qb$, $qb$Work longer without telling anyone and hope to recover$qb$, $qb$Wait until the deadline before explaining the problem$qb$, $qb$Blame the dependency that caused the delay$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R2-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realize your mistake may delay another person's task. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise it early, own the impact, correct it, and provide a realistic recovery plan.$qb$, $qb$Tell the affected person and correct the mistake quickly.$qb$, $qb$Fix it quietly and hope the delay can still be avoided.$qb$, $qb$Explain mainly what circumstances caused the mistake.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether the delay becomes a real problem.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You send the wrong version of a document and notice ten minutes later. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the recipient immediately, send the correct version, and state clearly which file to use.$qb$, $qb$Send the correct version with a short correction message.$qb$, $qb$Send the correct version without mentioning the earlier mistake.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether the wrong file gets used.$qb$, $qb$Do nothing because the mistake may never matter.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague tells you that your handoff was incomplete and delayed their work. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the gap, complete the handoff, and improve your checklist$qb$, $qb$Complete the missing part and apologize for the delay$qb$, $qb$Explain that the handoff format was not clearly defined$qb$, $qb$Ask what they could have done to spot the gap earlier$qb$, $qb$Say the delay was small and move on$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realize you misunderstood an instruction and completed the wrong version of a task. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the misunderstanding, clarify the requirement, and correct the work promptly$qb$, $qb$Tell the requester what happened and agree the correction$qb$, $qb$Correct the work without mentioning the misunderstanding$qb$, $qb$Explain that the instruction was unclear$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether the wrong version is accepted$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You tell a teammate a task is complete, then discover you missed one required check. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, complete the check, and correct any impact$qb$, $qb$Complete the check and update them if anything changes$qb$, $qb$Do the check quietly and say nothing if it passes$qb$, $qb$Wait until they use the work$qb$, $qb$Assume the check is unnecessary because the task looked correct$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You gave a colleague an incorrect date and they planned work around it. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the date immediately, acknowledge the impact, and help adjust the plan$qb$, $qb$Tell them the correct date and apologize$qb$, $qb$Update the date in the system without contacting them$qb$, $qb$Wait until they notice the change$qb$, $qb$Say the original date was only an estimate$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realize your late reply caused someone else to miss a small deadline. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the delay, help recover the impact, and change how you track such requests$qb$, $qb$Apologize, respond now, and agree the next step$qb$, $qb$Explain that you had other priorities$qb$, $qb$Say the missed deadline was minor$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the deadline has passed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cross-functional decision you chaired creates an unexpected cost in another division. The information was available but not surfaced during the meeting. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Own the decision process, correct the impact, and change the decision mechanism so material cross-division effects are surfaced earlier.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the cost was not raised by the affected division and therefore was not reasonably foreseeable.$qb$, $qb$Ask the affected division to absorb the cost this time while the process is improved.$qb$, $qb$Reopen the decision only if the cost exceeds the division's tolerance.$qb$, $qb$Shift accountability to the person who held the information but failed to mention it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A portfolio you lead missed a public commitment because you approved competing priorities that overloaded a shared team. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the prioritization failure, stabilize commitments, reset capacity and decision rules, and communicate what will change at portfolio level.$qb$, $qb$Ask the shared team why it did not flag overload earlier.$qb$, $qb$Apologize and ask project managers to recover their schedules.$qb$, $qb$Replace the planning tool with one showing resource conflicts.$qb$, $qb$Attribute the miss to unusually high demand across projects.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A public commitment you made will be missed because assumptions in your own plan were unrealistic, although teams warned early. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the planning judgment, acknowledge the warnings, reset transparently, and strengthen how assumptions are challenged and escalated.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business unit misses a target after you redirected resources to an enterprise emergency. Its leader is being criticized for the miss. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Make your resource decision and its consequences explicit, ensure performance is judged in that context, and own the enterprise trade-off rather than leaving the unit to absorb blame.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$At first, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$In this case, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-023$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$After years of volatility, the organization has become permanently cautious and slow even as market opportunity returns. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Reset risk appetite and decision pace deliberately, retain useful resilience disciplines, remove obsolete crisis constraints and rebuild confidence through bounded, accountable action.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You promised an update by noon and realize at 11:30 that it will not be ready. Other people have planned around your commitment. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the recipient before noon, give the reason, and provide a realistic new time$qb$, $qb$Tell them it will be late and give the new expected time$qb$, $qb$Send an incomplete update at noon to meet the promise$qb$, $qb$Wait until the work is finished before explaining the delay$qb$, $qb$Say nothing unless they ask$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A dependency you were responsible for tracking is late and now threatens the team's deadline. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Own the missed follow-up, raise the risk immediately, and lead a realistic recovery plan$qb$, $qb$Tell the team what is late and propose the next recovery step$qb$, $qb$Explain that the dependency owner caused the delay$qb$, $qb$Wait for the dependency before updating the team$qb$, $qb$Reduce the final quality so the original deadline can still be met$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You own a project risk that has increased, but earlier updates described it as low. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Update the risk honestly, explain what changed, assess the impact, and lead the revised mitigation rather than defending the earlier rating$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the risk has increased and propose stronger mitigation$qb$, $qb$Keep the old rating until the impact becomes clearer$qb$, $qb$Explain that the earlier rating was reasonable and leave ownership with the risk source$qb$, $qb$Wait for a manager to change the rating$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You learn a new shortcut that saves time, but it caused one small error on your first use. What is the best learning response?$qb$, $qb$Understand why the error occurred, adjust the method, and test it again safely before adopting it.$qb$, $qb$Review the error and add a check before using the shortcut again.$qb$, $qb$Stop using the shortcut permanently after one error.$qb$, $qb$Keep using it unchanged because it is faster overall.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the error because it was small.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You make the same small error twice in one month. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Identify why it repeated and change your working method$qb$, $qb$Review both cases and add a check to your routine$qb$, $qb$Be more careful next time$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to check all your work$qb$, $qb$Accept that occasional errors are normal$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive the same improvement suggestion from two different people. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Treat it as useful evidence, test a change, and check whether the result improves$qb$, $qb$Compare the two examples and adjust your next attempt$qb$, $qb$Thank both people but keep your current approach$qb$, $qb$Wait for a manager to give the same feedback$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because neither person is your manager$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive a correction on a task you have done many times. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Understand why the correction is needed and update your future approach$qb$, $qb$Ask for the reason and apply the correction next time$qb$, $qb$Make the correction only on this task$qb$, $qb$Defend your usual method because it worked before$qb$, $qb$Ignore the correction unless a manager repeats it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You learn that a method you use is producing more errors than a newer method. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Understand the newer method, practice it, and compare your results$qb$, $qb$Try the newer method on suitable tasks$qb$, $qb$Keep your method because you are faster with it$qb$, $qb$Use the new method only when someone checks$qb$, $qb$Ignore the evidence because your method usually works$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new task goes badly even though you prepared carefully. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review what happened, identify one or two lessons, and apply them to the next attempt$qb$, $qb$Ask for feedback and note what you would change next time$qb$, $qb$Avoid the task until you have more experience$qb$, $qb$Conclude that the task is not suited to you$qb$, $qb$Repeat the same approach because preparation was good$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You complete a task faster than before but make one new error. What is the best learning response?$qb$, $qb$Keep the useful speed improvement, understand the error, and adjust the method before repeating it$qb$, $qb$Review what caused the error and modify the faster method$qb$, $qb$Return completely to the old slower method$qb$, $qb$Ignore the error because overall speed improved$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster method unchanged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your organization is entering a field where your expertise is dated, yet colleagues defer to you because of your title. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Make your knowledge limits visible, bring in credible expertise, learn enough to challenge assumptions, and model changing your view when evidence warrants it.$qb$, $qb$Delegate all technical decisions to the newest specialists.$qb$, $qb$Take an executive course before allowing major decisions.$qb$, $qb$Continue leading from experience because strategic judgement matters more.$qb$, $qb$Avoid expressing views so experts are not influenced by hierarchy.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-026$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A successful internal venture is becoming large enough to reshape the company's identity and cannibalize core revenue. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate enterprise future value rather than protecting identity, preserve the venture's distinctive economics, plan cannibalization deliberately and redesign the portfolio as evidence strengthens.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You complete a task successfully using a new method. The lesson should improve future performance, not only this case. What is the best learning step?$qb$, $qb$Note what worked, what was difficult, and what you would repeat next time$qb$, $qb$Record the useful steps so you can use them again$qb$, $qb$Move on because the task was successful$qb$, $qb$Assume the method will work equally well in every case$qb$, $qb$Return to the old method because it is more familiar$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive feedback that a method you use works but creates avoidable work for others. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Understand the downstream impact, test a better method, and check whether the change improves the whole result$qb$, $qb$Ask affected colleagues for examples and adjust your method$qb$, $qb$Keep your method because your own output is correct$qb$, $qb$Change only when someone complains$qb$, $qb$Ask others to adapt their work around your method$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive feedback that your reports are hard to scan quickly. The learning should change future work, not only this example. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask for an example, change the format, and check whether it improves$qb$, $qb$Try a clearer format in your next report$qb$, $qb$Explain why the current format contains useful detail$qb$, $qb$Wait for the same feedback from another person$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the reports are technically correct$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague who is usually reliable seems upset and makes an unusual mistake. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check in privately and ask whether they need support$qb$, $qb$Ask what happened before discussing the mistake$qb$, $qb$Correct the mistake yourself and say nothing$qb$, $qb$Tell them they need to focus more carefully$qb$, $qb$Discuss their behaviour with other teammates$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate becomes quiet after receiving critical feedback. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check in privately and listen before offering advice$qb$, $qb$Ask whether they want to talk about the feedback$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take the feedback personally$qb$, $qb$Give them space and avoid the topic completely$qb$, $qb$Tell others they seem upset so the team can help$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says they are struggling with workload but continues meeting deadlines. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Listen, ask what is creating the pressure, and explore practical support$qb$, $qb$Ask which part of the workload is hardest$qb$, $qb$Tell them they seem to be managing well$qb$, $qb$Suggest they simply organize their time better$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because deadlines are being met$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate sounds frustrated after receiving several last-minute requests. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the pressure and ask what would help them handle the immediate priorities$qb$, $qb$Ask which request is creating the most difficulty$qb$, $qb$Tell them everyone is busy$qb$, $qb$Advise them to work faster$qb$, $qb$Avoid them until they calm down$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes defensive when you ask about a missed task. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Lower the tension, ask what happened, and listen before discussing the next step$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that the conversation may feel difficult and ask for the facts$qb$, $qb$Repeat that the task was missed$qb$, $qb$Tell them defensiveness is unhelpful$qb$, $qb$End the discussion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague makes a short, unusually sharp reply during a stressful day. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Avoid assuming intent, stay calm, and check whether there is an issue that needs attention$qb$, $qb$Respond calmly and ask whether everything is okay$qb$, $qb$Tell them their tone is unacceptable immediately$qb$, $qb$Reply sharply so they understand the impact$qb$, $qb$Complain about them to another colleague$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How should an employee apply emotional intelligence when a reliable peer demonstrates uncharacteristic quality drops?$qb$, $qb$Initiating a private, supportive check-in to express appreciation, understand challenges, and offer assistance.$qb$, $qb$Mentioning missed details quietly during routine work syncs to help the peer notice quality gaps.$qb$, $qb$Correcting the peer's work handoffs independently to maintain team deliverable quality without confrontation.$qb$, $qb$Informing the team lead about quality drops so workload can be reassigned through official channels.$qb$, $qb$Giving the peer space for several weeks, assuming work performance will self-correct over time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A manager who normally performs well has become abrupt with colleagues after a family crisis. Delivery is slipping and the team is beginning to react. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Address the behavior and impact privately with empathy, understand what support is needed, and agree near-term expectations that protect both the person and team.$qb$, $qb$Give the manager temporary flexibility and avoid discussing behavior until the crisis passes.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team to be patient because the manager is dealing with personal difficulty.$qb$, $qb$Move the manager's responsibilities to others without discussing the reason.$qb$, $qb$Start formal disciplinary action immediately because personal circumstances should not affect work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior colleague becomes unusually combative after losing responsibility in a reorganization and is damaging collaboration. What should their leader do?$qb$, $qb$Address observed behavior and impact directly, listen to their perspective, acknowledge legitimate loss without excusing conduct, and agree expectations and support.$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate pressure and document the remaining risk for later review.$qb$, $qb$Use a narrower solution that improves one dimension but leaves key dependencies unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the decision to another function without clarifying enterprise ownership.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current plan even when evidence shows its assumptions are weakening.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A peer whose role may be eliminated becomes withdrawn and stops contributing to decisions. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Speak privately without assumptions, acknowledge the uncertainty, understand what is affecting participation, and agree how they can remain meaningfully involved while decisions proceed.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A respected executive becomes unusually impatient after learning they were not selected for a larger role. Their team notices. What should their manager do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the observed change privately, listen to their perspective, separate disappointment from expected conduct, and agree support and behavioral expectations.$qb$, $qb$In this case, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$At first, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-025$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your board must decide on a scientific technology whose long-term societal consequences are uncertain and may extend beyond the company's lifetime. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Integrate scientific evidence, intergenerational and ethical consequences, independent expertise, staged commitments and long-term monitoring into the decision rather than relying on near-term commercial value.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague reacts strongly to a small change that seems easy to you. The relationship will matter after the immediate issue. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what concern the change creates for them before judging the reaction$qb$, $qb$Listen to their concern and explain what you know about the change$qb$, $qb$Tell them the change is minor and should not be a problem$qb$, $qb$Avoid them until they calm down$qb$, $qb$Tell others that the colleague is overreacting$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A usually cooperative colleague becomes resistant after a change affects work they value. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explore what they feel they are losing, acknowledge the concern, and discuss practical ways to address it$qb$, $qb$Ask what specifically about the change is difficult for them$qb$, $qb$Explain why the change is necessary and expect them to adapt$qb$, $qb$Avoid involving them until they become more positive$qb$, $qb$Tell them their resistance is affecting the team$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague who usually supports your work becomes distant after being excluded from an important discussion. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the exclusion, invite their perspective, understand the impact, and rebuild involvement where it still matters$qb$, $qb$Ask how the earlier decision affected them and what would improve collaboration now$qb$, $qb$Explain that the discussion had to happen quickly$qb$, $qb$Ignore the distance and focus on the work$qb$, $qb$Tell them they need to separate feelings from work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You finish your assigned work early and notice a small issue that may affect tomorrow's task. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate the issue and take a useful next step within your role$qb$, $qb$Flag the issue and offer to help resolve it$qb$, $qb$Wait for tomorrow because today's work is complete$qb$, $qb$Leave it for the person assigned tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Ignore it unless it becomes a larger problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A useful shared document contains an outdated link and you know the correct one. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Update it through the proper process or alert the owner with the exact correction.$qb$, $qb$Send the correct link to the owner and offer to update the document.$qb$, $qb$Use the correct link only for your own work.$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone reports that the link is broken.$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the document is not assigned to you.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A useful team document is outdated, and nobody has been assigned to update it. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the need and update it within your role, then share the change$qb$, $qb$Flag the outdated section and offer to update it$qb$, $qb$Wait for the document owner to notice$qb$, $qb$Create a separate personal version$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because it is not assigned to you$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A small customer question is sitting unassigned and you know the answer. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Answer it if it is within your role and make sure the request is properly recorded$qb$, $qb$Take ownership of the question and notify the usual owner$qb$, $qb$Wait for someone to assign it to you$qb$, $qb$Answer privately without updating the system$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because it is unassigned$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice that a recurring team reminder is no longer being sent because its owner left. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the reminder is still needed and help restore clear ownership$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the reminder has stopped and ask who should own it$qb$, $qb$Send the reminder yourself once$qb$, $qb$Wait for a manager to notice$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the former owner left$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice a small backlog building in an area you can help with after your priority work is complete. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check what help is useful, take an appropriate part, and keep the owner informed$qb$, $qb$Offer to help with a defined part of the backlog$qb$, $qb$Start working on random backlog items$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone assigns you work$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the backlog belongs to another person$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How can an associate best demonstrate enterprise during low-volume operational periods?$qb$, $qb$Pitching unprompted workflow enhancements after auditing internal documentation and identifying team bottlenecks.$qb$, $qb$Reaching out to team members casually to check if they require help with ongoing daily tasks.$qb$, $qb$Completing optional online learning modules available on the corporate training portal.$qb$, $qb$Organizing personal email folders and digital documents to prepare for upcoming workload cycles.$qb$, $qb$Remaining available on communication channels while awaiting new task assignments from supervisors.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An emerging customer segment fits no business unit's targets, but early evidence suggests substantial future value. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Sponsor a bounded cross-unit exploration with learning goals, ownership and decision gates, then use evidence to determine where it should scale.$qb$, $qb$Assign it to the unit with the closest existing customers.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the segment is large enough for a formal business case.$qb$, $qb$Ask Strategy to study it for the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Let interested teams pursue it independently with discretionary budgets.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A small internal team finds a revenue opportunity outside every division's plan; customers are enthusiastic but risks are unclear. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a bounded, accountable experiment with hypotheses, risk limits, funding and decision gates before deciding whether and where to scale.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An employee proposes a potentially valuable business model that conflicts with current channel economics. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Test the underlying customer and economic hypotheses in a bounded way, explicitly examine channel conflict, and set evidence-based gates before scaling.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team discovers that a service designed for enterprises is attracting small businesses through an unintended channel. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate the need and economics, test whether the channel can scale without damaging the core model, and use explicit evidence gates before formal expansion.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$At first, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$In this case, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice a simple improvement that could save your team a few minutes every day. The issue is recurring across more than one task. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Check that it is safe, suggest it, and help test it on a small scale$qb$, $qb$Share the idea with the person who owns the process$qb$, $qb$Start using it yourself without telling anyone$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone asks for improvement ideas$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the saving is small$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice a recurring small problem across several tasks, but fixing it is not formally assigned to anyone. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the pattern, identify the right owner, and take or propose a practical improvement within your authority$qb$, $qb$Document the pattern and offer to help the relevant owner address it$qb$, $qb$Fix individual cases whenever you see them$qb$, $qb$Wait for management to create an improvement project$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because ownership is unclear$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have several urgent tasks and notice you are starting to skip checks. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Reprioritize, protect the essential checks, and communicate any realistic delay.$qb$, $qb$Slow down enough to complete the highest-risk checks.$qb$, $qb$Continue quickly and review everything at the end.$qb$, $qb$Skip checks on smaller tasks.$qb$, $qb$Keep the same pace and hope no errors occur.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior person asks for an urgent update while you are handling another urgent issue. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$State what you are handling, confirm priorities, and give a realistic update time$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the request and provide the best timing you can$qb$, $qb$Drop the current issue immediately because the requester is senior$qb$, $qb$Try to answer both requests at once$qb$, $qb$Promise an immediate update even if you cannot deliver it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have a heavy workload and one task suddenly becomes more urgent. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reorder priorities, protect critical quality checks, and communicate any changed timing$qb$, $qb$Move the urgent task first and update people affected by the change$qb$, $qb$Work faster on everything$qb$, $qb$Add the urgent task without changing the plan$qb$, $qb$Skip checks so all tasks stay on time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are close to a deadline and notice your work quality is dropping. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Pause briefly, identify the essential remaining work, and protect the critical checks$qb$, $qb$Slow down and focus only on the highest-risk parts$qb$, $qb$Keep the same pace to meet the deadline$qb$, $qb$Skip all checks and review later$qb$, $qb$Submit unfinished work without explanation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Several people are waiting for your work and you feel pressure to send it before the final check. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Complete the critical check, communicate the realistic timing, and then send accurate work$qb$, $qb$Tell people you need a little more time for the final check$qb$, $qb$Send it now and check it afterward$qb$, $qb$Skip the check because several people are waiting$qb$, $qb$Send it without saying the check was skipped$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are under time pressure and two sources show different figures. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Resolve or clearly flag the difference before using the figure, even if the update takes slightly longer$qb$, $qb$Check the more reliable source and explain any remaining uncertainty$qb$, $qb$Choose the higher figure to be safe$qb$, $qb$Use whichever figure is easier to access$qb$, $qb$Average the figures without checking$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the most resilient response when a system outage occurs 1 hour prior to a major client submission deadline?$qb$, $qb$Disseminating clear status updates to clients with revised timelines while focusing on recovery coordination.$qb$, $qb$Answering incoming client messages individually as quickly as possible while technical teams work on recovery.$qb$, $qb$Focusing exclusively on technical recovery steps while deferring client communications until systems restore.$qb$, $qb$Forwarding client inquiries to department leadership so executive managers handle crisis communications.$qb$, $qb$Enabling automated out-of-office email replies informing clients that technical issues are being addressed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cyber incident is unresolved after 18 hours. The team is fatigued, executives want faster recovery, and a risky shortcut could restore service sooner. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Maintain clear risk thresholds, rotate exhausted roles, protect critical thinking, and use the shortcut only if an authorized owner accepts the residual risk.$qb$, $qb$Take the shortcut because prolonged outage risk is now more important than procedural risk.$qb$, $qb$Continue the current plan with the same core team because changing people may slow recovery.$qb$, $qb$Pause recovery until the team has rested sufficiently.$qb$, $qb$Delegate the shortcut decision entirely to the most senior engineer on the call.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$During a cyber incident, customer data may be exposed but confirmation could take hours. Disclosure has legal and reputational consequences. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Run disciplined incident command: protect containment, distinguish facts from hypotheses, use predefined disclosure thresholds, and update decision owners as evidence changes.$qb$, $qb$Wait for technical confirmation before involving executives.$qb$, $qb$Notify all customers immediately because transparency is safest.$qb$, $qb$Personally join the technical investigation to judge evidence.$qb$, $qb$Restore systems first and address disclosure afterward.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A prolonged operational crisis is entering its third week. The original response team knows the situation best but is visibly fatigued. What is the strongest decision?$qb$, $qb$Move to sustainable incident command with planned rotations, structured handovers and protected expert continuity.$qb$, $qb$Act on the strongest available signal while keeping a later review point.$qb$, $qb$Seek broader consensus before making a limited decision.$qb$, $qb$Use a familiar precedent even though important conditions differ.$qb$, $qb$Choose the easiest path to implement regardless of longer-term consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A crisis creates dozens of executive information requests that distract the small team solving the problem. What operating model is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Create one information and decision cadence, shield the resolution team, triage stakeholder needs, and rotate critical roles sustainably.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A prolonged restructuring is causing decision fatigue among leaders who must still run daily operations. What should the executive team do?$qb$, $qb$Reduce avoidable decision load, establish clear cadences and delegation, rotate intense responsibilities where possible, and protect recovery without losing critical control.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An extended integration has left key leaders exhausted, yet another major milestone is approaching. What should the executive sponsor do?$qb$, $qb$Reassess critical work, simplify governance, rotate or reinforce overloaded roles, and protect recovery while maintaining continuity on decisions that cannot pause.$qb$, $qb$At first, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$In this case, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-024$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A retiring executive's reputation is so strong that employees continue seeking their approval instead of the successor's. What should the CEO do?$qb$, $qb$Reinforce the successor's authority publicly and privately, redefine the retiring executive's role around counsel and transfer, and stop dual leadership from becoming normalized.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You make a small mistake while handling several urgent tasks. Pressure is high, but mistakes would create larger downstream work. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Correct it, check whether anything was affected, then reset your priorities$qb$, $qb$Correct it and slow down enough to avoid repeating it$qb$, $qb$Correct it quickly and continue at the same pace$qb$, $qb$Hide the mistake so it does not create more pressure$qb$, $qb$Blame the workload for the error$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Several urgent issues arrive during a high-risk task that requires careful checking. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Protect the critical checks, triage the new issues by impact, and reset expectations where necessary$qb$, $qb$Finish the highest-risk check and communicate when you can address the other issues$qb$, $qb$Handle the newest urgent issue first$qb$, $qb$Move quickly between all issues so everyone sees progress$qb$, $qb$Skip checks to keep response times low$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A sudden workload surge is causing the team to rush and error rates are rising. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Triage by impact, reduce or sequence lower-value work, protect critical controls, and communicate capacity limits early$qb$, $qb$Identify the highest-risk activities and temporarily reduce lower-priority commitments$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to work faster while checking each other's work$qb$, $qb$Keep all commitments and correct mistakes later$qb$, $qb$Relax quality checks until the surge is over$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that an automated reporting script you set up missed logging client transactions for two days, leading to incomplete billing records. What action do you take?$qb$, $qb$Take immediate ownership, inform affected stakeholders, fix the script bug, manually reconcile the missing billing records, and audit systems.$qb$, $qb$Fix the script bug immediately and manually add the missing billing records before anyone notices the error.$qb$, $qb$Report the script error to IT support, asking them to restore missing billing records from server backup logs.$qb$, $qb$Inform your supervisor that a software glitch caused tracking gaps and request assistance in notifying billing.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see if the finance department flags billing discrepancies during monthly audits before making manual adjustments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-068$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a tracking rule you configured incorrectly has caused several tasks to bypass review for three days. No serious error has yet been found. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the configuration error, stop further bypasses, identify affected tasks, inform the right stakeholders, review the exposure, and correct the rule and control gap.$qb$, $qb$Fix the rule immediately and check the most recent tasks yourself.$qb$, $qb$Ask the system administrator to investigate because the issue is technical.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the next scheduled quality audit to identify any affected tasks.$qb$, $qb$Correct the rule quietly because no serious error has occurred.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1124$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize you gave a teammate an outdated instruction and they have already completed part of the work using it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, own the outdated guidance, assess what work is affected, and help recover it while correcting the source you relied on.$qb$, $qb$Send the new instruction and let them work out the difference.$qb$, $qb$Wait until they finish so you do not disrupt them twice.$qb$, $qb$Explain that they should have checked the latest source themselves.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to tell them because you gave the wrong instruction.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-114$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You promised to coordinate an approval but forgot to follow up, and the delay now threatens a milestone. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the missed follow-up, contact the approver immediately, inform affected people, propose recovery options, and improve how you track critical dependencies.$qb$, $qb$Contact the approver urgently and avoid mentioning that you forgot.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the approver has not responded.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to take over the approval.$qb$, $qb$Wait another day because repeated follow-up may annoy the approver.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1170$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You promised a stakeholder a response by noon but realize at 11:50 that you cannot finish the analysis accurately. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Contact them before the deadline, explain what is complete and what remains, give a realistic revised time, and provide any safe interim information they need.$qb$, $qb$Send a rushed answer by noon.$qb$, $qb$Miss the deadline and explain afterward.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to finish the analysis without context.$qb$, $qb$Send no response until the analysis is perfect.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1262$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a spreadsheet you sent contains an incorrect filter and therefore omitted several records. A colleague has already started reviewing it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague immediately, send the corrected complete file, explain what was omitted, and help determine whether any review work must be repeated.$qb$, $qb$Send the corrected file without mentioning the omission.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the colleague finishes reviewing the first file.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to add the missing records themselves.$qb$, $qb$Say the records were added later rather than admitting the filter error.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1492$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a meeting invitation you sent gives the wrong building, and several external attendees may already be travelling. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Contact attendees immediately through the available channels, give the correct location and practical recovery information, own the error, and update the invitation.$qb$, $qb$Correct the calendar invitation and assume everyone will see it.$qb$, $qb$Wait for attendees to call if they cannot find the room.$qb$, $qb$Say the venue changed at short notice.$qb$, $qb$Ask reception to redirect anyone who arrives at the wrong building.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1630$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a calendar invitation you created sends visitors to the wrong campus entrance, and the meeting begins in less than an hour. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notify the stakeholder, send a corrected clearly versioned file, own the mistake, and improve the final-file check.$qb$, $qb$Send the corrected file without mentioning the issue.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether they notice.$qb$, $qb$Say the placeholder was intentional.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to ignore that section.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-204$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize you failed to follow up an action you accepted in a meeting, and another colleague is now waiting for your input. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Contact the colleague immediately, acknowledge that you missed the follow-up, provide the input or a realistic time for it, and improve your action-tracking method.$qb$, $qb$Send the input now and apologize briefly for the delay.$qb$, $qb$Complete the input before saying anything so the colleague receives a finished answer.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the meeting contained many actions and yours was easy to miss.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the colleague reminds you again because they may no longer need it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-250$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague tells you that a mistake in a shared file came from a section you updated, although nobody can yet see exactly when it happened. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review your change history promptly, acknowledge your possible contribution, help correct the file, and establish the cause before deciding what prevention is needed.$qb$, $qb$Correct your section and wait for others to investigate the full cause.$qb$, $qb$Say you will accept responsibility only if the audit history proves the error was yours.$qb$, $qb$Ask the file owner to determine who caused the mistake before you spend time fixing it.$qb$, $qb$Defend your update because the file passed review after you changed it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2819$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You told a colleague you had sent a file, then realize it remained in your drafts and their work is now delayed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send it immediately, acknowledge your mistake and its impact, help recover the delay, and improve your send/follow-up check.$qb$, $qb$Send it now without mentioning the mistake.$qb$, $qb$Say the email system must have delayed it.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to send their copy.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the colleague asks again.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-342$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You give a teammate incorrect information that causes them to spend an hour on unnecessary work. You discover the mistake before they mention it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, own the incorrect information, help recover any remaining impact, and correct the source or checking step that led you to give the wrong answer.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the information has changed and redirect them to the correct work.$qb$, $qb$Wait until they finish their current work so the interruption does not waste even more time.$qb$, $qb$Correct the source and avoid mentioning your mistake unless they ask.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because only an hour was lost and the project is still on schedule.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-388$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You agree to coordinate a small task but assume someone else will schedule the meeting. Two days later, you discover nobody scheduled it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the coordination gap, schedule the meeting promptly, tell affected people about any impact, and make explicit who owns logistical actions in future.$qb$, $qb$Schedule the meeting now without discussing why it was missed.$qb$, $qb$Ask the person you thought would schedule it to do so immediately.$qb$, $qb$Explain that coordination did not necessarily include scheduling.$qb$, $qb$Wait until your manager decides who should have scheduled it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-434$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a task you marked complete yesterday actually has one small unfinished action that you overlooked. Nobody else has noticed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reopen or correct the status, complete or assign the remaining action, and tell affected people if the incorrect completion status influenced their work.$qb$, $qb$Complete the small action quietly and leave the status unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone asks about the unfinished action.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to complete it so the task can remain marked complete.$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the task is substantially finished.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-480$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You tell a stakeholder that a task is complete, then discover that you misunderstood the completion criteria. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the stakeholder promptly, explain what remains, provide a realistic completion plan, and clarify the criteria so the same misunderstanding does not recur.$qb$, $qb$Finish the remaining work quickly before correcting the stakeholder.$qb$, $qb$Tell the stakeholder that the task is technically complete but one interpretation differs.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager whether the remaining work is important enough to reopen the task.$qb$, $qb$Leave the status unchanged because the stakeholder has already been told it is complete.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-664$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize that a reminder you promised to send was never sent, and a colleague consequently missed an internal deadline. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague and affected owner promptly, acknowledge the missed reminder, help recover the deadline impact, and replace the informal reminder with a reliable tracking mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Send the reminder now and apologize to the colleague.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the colleague was still responsible for remembering their own deadline.$qb$, $qb$Ask for the deadline to be extended without mentioning the missed reminder.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because reminders are only a courtesy.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-940$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You accidentally book a courier collection for the wrong day and discover the mistake after the recipient has arranged staff to receive it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, own the error, help find the least-disruptive correction, and fix the source that carried the wrong date.$qb$, $qb$Correct the calendar silently.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see if they notice.$qb$, $qb$Say the date changed.$qb$, $qb$Ask an administrator to handle it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project report you submitted receives significant critical feedback from a senior stakeholder regarding analytical depth and data framing. How do you react?$qb$, $qb$View the feedback as a development opportunity, schedule a quick follow-up to clarify expectations, and rebuild the report incorporating deeper analysis.$qb$, $qb$Thank the stakeholder for the feedback and make the specific line-item revisions requested in the report.$qb$, $qb$Explain to the stakeholder the time constraints under which the initial report was produced to contextualize the depth level.$qb$, $qb$Ask a senior peer to review your updated draft before resubmitting it to ensure it meets stakeholder standards.$qb$, $qb$Accept the feedback quietly and rely on established standard templates for future reports to minimize deviation risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1127$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You use a new technique successfully on one task, but the result depends on a setting you do not fully understand. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Learn what the setting controls, test the technique under a few relevant conditions, and document when it is safe to use before applying it routinely.$qb$, $qb$Keep the successful settings exactly the same for every future task.$qb$, $qb$Use the technique broadly because the first result was good.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to choose the setting every time.$qb$, $qb$Return permanently to the old technique.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-117$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You repeat an error that you believed you had fixed after earlier feedback. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare both incidents, identify why the earlier correction did not change the underlying habit or process, seek targeted feedback, and install a stronger preventive check.$qb$, $qb$Apologize and promise to be more careful next time.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to check all similar work permanently.$qb$, $qb$Blame workload because the error happened during a busy period.$qb$, $qb$Avoid similar tasks until you have more experience.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1173$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn a shortcut that makes a task much faster, but you later discover it works only for one type of case. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the conditions where the shortcut is valid, update your working guidance, and use the full method for cases outside those conditions.$qb$, $qb$Stop using the shortcut completely.$qb$, $qb$Keep using it until an error occurs.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to decide case by case for you.$qb$, $qb$Use the shortcut for all simple-looking cases without defining the boundary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1265$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn a new reporting technique that produces clearer charts, but it takes much longer than your current method. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which parts improve decision quality, practice the technique to reduce effort, and use it where the added clarity justifies the time rather than adopting or rejecting it wholesale.$qb$, $qb$Use it for every report because the charts are better.$qb$, $qb$Return to the old method because it is faster.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to create all charts.$qb$, $qb$Use the new method only when senior leaders will see the report.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1311$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn a new shortcut for preparing a report, but it hides some intermediate calculations that helped you detect errors before. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Understand how the shortcut handles those calculations, add an appropriate validation check, and use it only when you can preserve the necessary error detection.$qb$, $qb$Use the shortcut because the final report looks correct.$qb$, $qb$Reject the shortcut and keep the old method permanently.$qb$, $qb$Use the shortcut only when deadlines are tight.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to check every report after you use it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1449$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn a faster spreadsheet technique but discover it behaves differently when filtered rows are present. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Learn how the technique treats filtered rows, test representative cases, and use it only where the behavior matches the intended calculation.$qb$, $qb$Use it because it is faster.$qb$, $qb$Stop using it entirely.$qb$, $qb$Unfilter every sheet before using it.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to verify every result.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1495$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn a useful keyboard shortcut that speeds a task, but you notice it sometimes applies changes to hidden rows. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Understand when hidden rows are affected, test the shortcut safely, and use it only with a check that prevents unintended changes.$qb$, $qb$Keep using it because most files have no hidden rows.$qb$, $qb$Stop using keyboard shortcuts.$qb$, $qb$Unhide every row before using it, regardless of need.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to inspect files afterward.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1541$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn a faster way to reconcile data, but it assumes there are no duplicate records. Your dataset sometimes contains duplicates. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Understand how duplicates affect the method, add a duplicate check or valid handling step, and use the faster approach only when its assumption is controlled.$qb$, $qb$Use it because duplicates are uncommon.$qb$, $qb$Stop using the faster method entirely.$qb$, $qb$Delete all duplicate-looking records first.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to reconcile datasets with duplicates.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1587$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn a new formula that saves time, but it gives unexpected results when cells contain text such as 'N/A.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Understand how the formula handles text values, test representative inputs, and add appropriate validation or handling before using it routinely.$qb$, $qb$Use it because most cells contain numbers.$qb$, $qb$Replace every 'N/A' with zero.$qb$, $qb$Stop using the formula.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to review only files containing text.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-161$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A junior colleague shows you a faster way to complete a task, and initial results appear as accurate as your usual method. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the method, test it on suitable examples, adopt it if reliable, and acknowledge their contribution when sharing the improvement.$qb$, $qb$Try the method on your next task and keep using it if the result looks correct.$qb$, $qb$Thank the colleague but continue your established method until a manager formally recommends the change.$qb$, $qb$Use the method privately so you gain the benefit without disrupting team practice.$qb$, $qb$Dismiss it because someone with less experience may not understand risks you have already learned.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-207$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive the same feedback about your written updates for the second time in three months. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for specific examples, compare them with the earlier feedback, identify the repeated pattern, practice a changed format, and seek follow-up feedback on improvement.$qb$, $qb$Change your next update based on what you remember from the feedback.$qb$, $qb$Take a writing course when a formal training opportunity appears.$qb$, $qb$Explain that different readers prefer different styles and continue your current approach.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the repeated feedback because your updates still contain the required information.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-253$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You complete a task successfully but realize you relied heavily on a colleague for a part you are expected to handle independently in future. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the knowledge gap, ask the colleague to explain the reasoning rather than simply repeat the solution, practice the task yourself, and verify that you can perform it independently next time.$qb$, $qb$Keep notes from what the colleague did and use them as a guide next time.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to remain available the next time you perform the task.$qb$, $qb$Wait for formal training because learning informally may create inconsistent practice.$qb$, $qb$Continue relying on the colleague because the work is being completed accurately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2846$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You solve a new type of problem after a colleague gives you the answer, but you do not understand why it worked. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Revisit the reasoning, reproduce the solution on a comparable example, and identify when the approach would or would not apply before treating it as learned.$qb$, $qb$Save the answer for reuse next time.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to handle similar problems.$qb$, $qb$Assume understanding will come with repetition.$qb$, $qb$Take another general course instead of reviewing this case.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-299$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You perform poorly on a task you expected to handle easily because the work required a skill you have not used for a long time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the specific skill gap, refresh it through targeted practice or guidance, apply it again on a suitable task, and check whether your performance improves.$qb$, $qb$Review the task and try to do better the next time it appears.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to handle that part of future tasks because they are more current.$qb$, $qb$Wait for formal refresher training before using the skill again.$qb$, $qb$Conclude that the skill is no longer relevant to your role because you rarely use it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-437$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You complete a course on a new skill but have no immediate project requiring it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a small realistic practice task, seek feedback on the result, and record where the skill could be applied so the learning is reinforced before it fades.$qb$, $qb$Keep the course notes and review them when a real task appears.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to assign you a project that requires the skill immediately.$qb$, $qb$Take another course on the same topic to strengthen your knowledge.$qb$, $qb$Assume the skill will return naturally when you need it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-483$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive a strong performance result on a task but feedback says your method would not work well at larger scale. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Understand which assumptions break at larger scale, learn or test a more scalable approach, and compare it with your current method before the next higher-volume task.$qb$, $qb$Keep your current method until you actually receive a larger task.$qb$, $qb$Ask for an example of a scalable method and copy it next time.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the feedback because the current result was successful.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone with more experience to handle future large-scale tasks.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-529$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You solve an unfamiliar problem after several attempts, but you are not sure which change actually fixed it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reconstruct the attempts, identify the likely causal change, document what you learned, and test the understanding safely so the solution can be repeated rather than remembered as trial and error.$qb$, $qb$Save the final working version and reuse it if the problem appears again.$qb$, $qb$Ask a more experienced colleague what they think probably fixed it.$qb$, $qb$Move on because the problem is solved and further analysis has little value.$qb$, $qb$Document every attempted change without deciding which one mattered.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A typically reliable colleague appears distracted, missing minor details in recent team handoffs. You suspect personal stress is impacting their work. How do you approach them?$qb$, $qb$Initiate a private, supportive check-in, express appreciation for their usual work, inquire gently how you can support, and offer temporary handoff help.$qb$, $qb$Mention the missed handoff details quietly during your next work sync and ask if they need help catching up.$qb$, $qb$Adjust their submitted handoffs yourself to maintain deliverable quality without bringing up performance issues.$qb$, $qb$Inform your team lead about the missed details so workload can be adjusted officially.$qb$, $qb$Give them space for a week, assuming their work quality will return to baseline once personal stress subsides.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-116$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes unusually quiet after their proposal is rejected in a meeting. You need their cooperation on the next phase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check in privately, acknowledge the disappointment without assuming how they feel, listen to their perspective, and reconnect them to meaningful next-phase contribution.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take the rejection personally.$qb$, $qb$Give them space and avoid involving them for a few days.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to find out what is wrong.$qb$, $qb$Remind them that the team decision is final and they must support it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1172$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague who made an error says, 'I knew I would mess this up,' and appears embarrassed. The error needs correction today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Address the specific error and recovery calmly, avoid reinforcing the self-judgment, and help them identify the practical cause and next prevention step.$qb$, $qb$Reassure them they never make mistakes and do the correction yourself.$qb$, $qb$Tell them confidence is the main problem.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the error and ignore the comment.$qb$, $qb$Agree they need more training before doing similar work again.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1310$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes upset after a customer cancels work they spent a week preparing. Some of the preparation can be reused elsewhere. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the disappointment, identify what work can be reused, and help redirect effort while avoiding dismissive comments about the time already spent.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to worry because the work can be reused.$qb$, $qb$Focus immediately on finding another customer for the work.$qb$, $qb$Let them abandon all related work for the day.$qb$, $qb$Tell them cancellations are a normal part of business.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1402$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague receives a short, blunt message from a manager and assumes they are in trouble. You know the manager often writes briefly when busy. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the colleague's concern, avoid claiming to know the manager's intent, and help them focus on the actual request or seek clarification if needed.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to worry because the manager always writes that way.$qb$, $qb$Agree that the message sounds angry.$qb$, $qb$Ask other colleagues whether the manager is upset.$qb$, $qb$Advise them to apologize immediately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1540$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is upset because their work was heavily edited by a reviewer. Some edits are necessary, while others are style preferences. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the reaction, separate required changes from preferences, and help them focus on the substantive improvements without treating every edit as a failure.$qb$, $qb$Tell them reviewers always have the final say.$qb$, $qb$Reject all style edits to protect the colleague.$qb$, $qb$Redo the document yourself.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to be emotionally attached to their work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1632$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate has spent days preparing analysis that is no longer needed because leadership changed direction. They are visibly disappointed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the reaction and help retain necessary detail while making the recommendation and decision path easier to find.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to cut the document in half.$qb$, $qb$Agree the reviewer simply dislikes their style.$qb$, $qb$Remove all supporting detail.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take feedback personally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-206$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes unusually defensive when you ask about a missed deadline. You need to understand what happened without worsening the conversation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep your tone calm, describe the missed deadline without judging the person, ask what affected it, listen, and then agree the recovery and future expectation.$qb$, $qb$Ask directly why they are being defensive before discussing the deadline.$qb$, $qb$Give them time to calm down and revisit the deadline next week.$qb$, $qb$Explain the consequences of the missed deadline so they understand why you are concerned.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the behavior to their manager because defensiveness prevents useful discussion.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-252$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague reacts strongly when a small part of their work is criticized in a group review. You need to continue reviewing the rest of the work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the reaction without judging it, bring the discussion back to the specific work point, keep the tone respectful, and offer a private follow-up if more conversation is needed.$qb$, $qb$Pause the review and ask the colleague to explain why the comment upset them in front of the group.$qb$, $qb$Skip all remaining feedback on their work to avoid making the situation worse.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague the criticism was minor and they should not take it personally.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to continue the review because the colleague is too emotional.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2821$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is frustrated because a customer rejected work they spent days preparing. You need to discuss what must be redone. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the disappointment, separate effort from the customer's valid requirements, and agree the necessary rework and support without minimizing their effort.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the customer is always right and the work must be redone.$qb$, $qb$Avoid discussing rework until they feel better.$qb$, $qb$Redo the work yourself so they do not feel worse.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on how much time was wasted.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-298$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague who made a visible mistake keeps apologizing repeatedly even after the issue has been corrected. You need them focused on the next task. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the mistake and correction, reassure them that the immediate issue is closed, ask what they learned, and redirect attention to the next task without minimizing the impact.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to stop apologizing because everyone makes mistakes.$qb$, $qb$Give them easier work for the rest of the day so they can regain confidence.$qb$, $qb$Keep discussing the mistake until you are sure they understand its seriousness.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the apologies and assign the next task without comment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-344$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague seems irritated when you ask them for help, but they still agree to assist. You do not know why they are irritated. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that they may be under pressure, ask whether the timing works or another arrangement would be better, and avoid assuming the irritation is about you.$qb$, $qb$Ask why they are annoyed with you before accepting their help.$qb$, $qb$Accept the help and ignore their mood because they agreed.$qb$, $qb$Withdraw the request immediately and find someone else.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they should say no directly instead of showing irritation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-390$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague receives unexpected extra work and responds with a sarcastic comment in a meeting. You need their cooperation on the task. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that the added work may be frustrating, clarify the priority and workload impact, and discuss what support or trade-off is realistic without reacting to the sarcasm.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague the sarcasm is unprofessional before discussing the work.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the comment and repeat the instruction.$qb$, $qb$Take the work away from them because their reaction suggests they are unwilling.$qb$, $qb$Report the comment to their manager so expectations are reinforced.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-482$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is unusually short with you during a busy period. Later they apologize and say they are under pressure. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Accept the apology, acknowledge the pressure without excusing repeated disrespect, and discuss any practical coordination change that could reduce friction while work remains busy.$qb$, $qb$Tell them no apology is needed because everyone gets stressed.$qb$, $qb$Accept the apology but avoid working closely with them until the busy period ends.$qb$, $qb$Explain that pressure is not an excuse and ask them to control their behavior.$qb$, $qb$Report the incident so there is a record if it happens again.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-528$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague who is usually confident asks you to double-check a simple task after receiving critical feedback earlier in the day. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check what they are uncertain about, provide proportionate reassurance or review, and help them reconnect the task to clear criteria rather than treating the temporary loss of confidence as incompetence.$qb$, $qb$Review the entire task carefully because their confidence is clearly affected.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they know how to do the task and should trust themselves.$qb$, $qb$Take the task over so they can recover from the difficult feedback.$qb$, $qb$Tell their manager that the feedback has affected their performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-574$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague who has just returned from leave seems overwhelmed by accumulated requests and asks which one you need first. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify your genuine priority, acknowledge the volume they are returning to, and avoid presenting everything as urgent while helping identify any dependency your request creates.$qb$, $qb$Tell them all your requests are important and let them decide the order.$qb$, $qb$Withdraw your requests entirely until they catch up.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager to prioritize their backlog.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they should have arranged better cover before taking leave.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-620$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague misses a small commitment and immediately explains that they are dealing with several personal pressures. You still need the work today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the pressure without probing into private details, clarify what is realistically possible today, and agree support or a revised handoff that protects the essential work.$qb$, $qb$Tell them personal issues should not affect agreed commitments.$qb$, $qb$Take the work away from them without further discussion.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to take the day off and postpone the work.$qb$, $qb$Ask for details about the personal pressures so you can judge whether the delay is justified.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-666$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague reacts negatively when you reassign part of their work to help meet a deadline. They say it feels like you do not trust them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge how the reassignment may feel, explain the capacity and deadline reason, listen to their concern, and clarify that the change is about workload rather than confidence in their ability.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take the reassignment personally.$qb$, $qb$Return all the work to them so they feel trusted.$qb$, $qb$Explain that deadlines matter more than feelings during busy periods.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager to reassure them that their performance is fine.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-712$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is disappointed that someone else was selected for an opportunity they wanted. You were involved in the selection. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the disappointment, explain the job-relevant selection criteria without becoming defensive, listen to their questions, and discuss credible development steps for future opportunities.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the selected person was simply stronger this time.$qb$, $qb$Apologize for disappointing them even though the selection was fair.$qb$, $qb$Avoid discussing the decision because they may become more upset.$qb$, $qb$Promise them the next opportunity so they remain motivated.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-804$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague who normally responds quickly has missed two messages during a difficult week. You need an answer today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send a concise follow-up stating the genuine deadline, acknowledge they may be busy, and offer an easier response route or alternative contact without assuming why they are unavailable.$qb$, $qb$Send repeated reminders until they respond.$qb$, $qb$Tell their manager they are not responding.$qb$, $qb$Assume they are overwhelmed and remove the task from them.$qb$, $qb$Wait silently because following up may add pressure.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a quiet operational week with minimal incoming tasks, how do you spend your available work hours?$qb$, $qb$Identify recurring team process bottlenecks, audit internal documentation for updates, and pitch a small workflow enhancement to your lead.$qb$, $qb$Reach out to team members to ask if they need assistance with their ongoing deliverables.$qb$, $qb$Complete optional online training modules available on the company learning portal.$qb$, $qb$Organize your personal digital files and clear unread emails to prepare for upcoming workload cycles.$qb$, $qb$Remain available on team chat channels while waiting for new task assignments from your supervisor.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
