  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-300$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring team meeting has no owner for maintaining its action log, and actions are sometimes forgotten. You are not the meeting lead. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise the gap, propose a simple ownership rule or rotating role, and help establish it rather than silently taking permanent responsibility yourself.$qb$, $qb$Start maintaining the action log yourself every week.$qb$, $qb$Remind the meeting lead whenever an action appears to be forgotten.$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager the meeting is poorly organized and wait for them to fix it.$qb$, $qb$Do nothing because meeting administration belongs to the meeting lead.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-346$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a shared folder has become difficult to use because files are named inconsistently. No one owns folder organization. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Propose a simple naming convention, test it on a small set, get agreement from regular users, and organize future files without deleting or renaming important records blindly.$qb$, $qb$Rename all files yourself using the convention you think is clearest.$qb$, $qb$Create your own organized subfolder and use it for future work.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the folder is messy and ask someone to volunteer to fix it.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because file organization is an administrative responsibility.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
