  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-622$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a shared meeting room is repeatedly double-booked because two calendars are being used. No one has been assigned to fix the issue. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm which calendar should be authoritative, involve facilities or the relevant owner, and help move users to one booking source so the conflict is removed at system level.$qb$, $qb$Check both calendars before every booking and warn colleagues when you see conflicts.$qb$, $qb$Create a third combined calendar for your team.$qb$, $qb$Tell facilities that the room is often double-booked and wait for them to act.$qb$, $qb$Use another room whenever a conflict happens.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-668$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a recurring meeting starts late because participants spend the first few minutes locating the same documents. No one owns preparation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create or propose one pre-meeting location containing the current documents, assign a simple preparation owner or routine, and test whether meetings start more effectively.$qb$, $qb$Open all the documents yourself before every meeting.$qb$, $qb$Tell participants to arrive five minutes early to find their files.$qb$, $qb$Ask the meeting chair to fix the problem.$qb$, $qb$Accept the delay because it is only a few minutes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
