  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-806$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring request arrives through several channels, and colleagues spend time checking whether it has already been handled. No one owns intake design. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Propose one visible intake point or deduplication method, involve regular users, and test whether it removes repeated checking before making it standard.$qb$, $qb$Create your own list of handled requests.$qb$, $qb$Tell colleagues to search all channels before acting.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to appoint someone to monitor duplicates.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current channels because different requesters prefer different methods.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An unexpected system outage occurs 1 hour before a major client deadline. Incoming messages from anxious clients are filling your inbox. How do you handle the pressure?$qb$, $qb$Maintain composure, send a clear status broadcast to clients acknowledging the issue and providing a revised timeline, and focus on recovery steps.$qb$, $qb$Respond individually to incoming client messages as fast as possible to reassure them while IT works on system recovery.$qb$, $qb$Focus entirely on assisting IT with technical recovery, deferring client messaging until system operations are restored.$qb$, $qb$Forward urgent client messages to your department manager so executive leadership can handle client communications.$qb$, $qb$Send an automated out-of-office message informing clients that technical issues are being addressed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1125$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An urgent customer problem remains unresolved, and the team has been working for hours. People begin skipping documentation to move faster. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep essential incident records concise but current, assign documentation if possible, and prevent fatigue-driven speed from destroying the evidence needed for safe diagnosis and handover.$qb$, $qb$Stop documentation until the problem is solved.$qb$, $qb$Require full normal documentation after every action even if it slows response heavily.$qb$, $qb$Let each person decide what is worth recording.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to accept that records may be incomplete during emergencies.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1171$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A critical issue occurs while your manager is unavailable. Two possible actions are allowed by procedure, but one is faster and carries more reversible risk. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the immediate impact, reversibility and evidence, choose the proportionate authorized action, document the reasoning, and update the manager when available.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the manager because the decision is important.$qb$, $qb$Choose the faster option automatically.$qb$, $qb$Choose the slower option because it sounds safer.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to choose which action they prefer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1263$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A high-pressure issue is taking longer than expected, and a senior stakeholder demands a guaranteed resolution time that the team cannot support. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain what is known, give the best evidence-based range or next diagnostic milestone, and commit to update points rather than inventing a guarantee.$qb$, $qb$Give the most likely resolution time as a firm commitment.$qb$, $qb$Refuse to discuss timing at all.$qb$, $qb$Ask the technical team to choose a time that will satisfy the stakeholder.$qb$, $qb$Promise an optimistic time and revise it later if necessary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1309$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a busy operational disruption, several small issues occur at once and the team starts treating all of them as equally urgent. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Prioritize by safety, customer and operational impact, assign ownership, and keep lower-impact issues visible without allowing them to distract from the critical path.$qb$, $qb$Work on whichever issue was reported first.$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to choose their own highest priority.$qb$, $qb$Treat every issue as critical until resolved.$qb$, $qb$Ignore small issues completely until the main disruption ends.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1401$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A major deadline is approaching and a colleague proposes skipping a backup step because the primary process has worked all day. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess what the backup step protects and the consequence of failure, preserve it if material, and find time savings in lower-risk work instead.$qb$, $qb$Skip it because the primary process is currently stable.$qb$, $qb$Keep every normal step unchanged regardless of urgency.$qb$, $qb$Ask the most senior person present whether to skip it.$qb$, $qb$Do the backup step after the deadline.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1447$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During an urgent outage, several people start investigating the same symptom while another critical symptom has no owner. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assign owners by impact and diagnostic need so the unowned critical symptom is covered and duplicate investigation is reduced.$qb$, $qb$Let everyone continue investigating what they noticed first.$qb$, $qb$Tell the most senior person to investigate everything.$qb$, $qb$Stop all work until one cause is agreed.$qb$, $qb$Ignore smaller symptoms entirely.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-159$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A system issue, an urgent customer request, and your scheduled deadline all need attention at the same time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess immediate impact, communicate priorities and expected timing, work deliberately in sequence, and seek help if the total demand exceeds your capacity.$qb$, $qb$Handle the system issue first because technical incidents usually affect more people, then reassess the other work.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to take the customer request while you continue your planned deadline.$qb$, $qb$Switch frequently between all three tasks so each one shows progress.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the original deadline because it was committed before the other issues arose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-205$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer escalation arrives near the end of a difficult day when you are tired and still have one important task to finish. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the escalation's immediate impact, communicate a realistic response time, take a brief reset if safe, and sequence it with the remaining task rather than reacting from fatigue.$qb$, $qb$Handle the escalation immediately and finish your planned task afterward, even if that means working late.$qb$, $qb$Finish the planned task first because it was already scheduled.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to take the escalation without checking whether they have the context.$qb$, $qb$Respond quickly while tired so the customer knows the issue has attention.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-251$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a busy morning, two people repeatedly ask when your work will be finished while you are trying to resolve the issue causing the delay. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Give both people a realistic update and next check-in time, then protect a focused period to resolve the issue unless new information changes the priority.$qb$, $qb$Reply to every request immediately so stakeholders remain reassured.$qb$, $qb$Ignore all messages until the issue is fully resolved.$qb$, $qb$Give an optimistic completion time so people stop asking for updates.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to handle all stakeholder communication while you work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2820$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A system incident occurs just before a major deadline. Team members start making several changes at once to recover faster. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Establish controlled change ownership, test one evidence-based intervention at a time where practical, and record changes so the team can learn what actually affects the incident.$qb$, $qb$Allow parallel changes because speed matters most.$qb$, $qb$Stop all changes until the deadline passes.$qb$, $qb$Let the most senior person make changes without recording them.$qb$, $qb$Restart the system repeatedly until the problem clears.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
