  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-FIB-016$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$If you cannot meet a deadline, telling people early is worth more than working ______.$qb$, $qb$silently$qb$, $qb$publicly$qb$, $qb$formally$qb$, $qb$casually$qb$, $qb$remotely$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-FIB-017$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$FIB$qb$, $qb$When a shared task fails, asking what you personally could have done differently is more useful than asking who was ______.$qb$, $qb$responsible$qb$, $qb$paid$qb$, $qb$informed$qb$, $qb$present$qb$, $qb$absent$qb$, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-018$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You sent a customer the wrong order. You spot it before they do. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer now, explain what went wrong, and say when the right order will arrive$qb$, $qb$Send the correct order straight away and tell the customer what happened as you do$qb$, $qb$Tell your supervisor immediately so they can decide how the customer is contacted$qb$, $qb$Send the correct order quickly and hope it arrives before the customer notices$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether the customer contacts you, since they may not have opened it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-019$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A report you signed off contained a figure that turned out to be wrong. Someone else prepared it. What is your position?$qb$, $qb$You are answerable for it, because signing it off was your check to make$qb$, $qb$You share responsibility with the preparer, since both steps failed here$qb$, $qb$You should correct it and look at why the check did not catch the error$qb$, $qb$The preparer is responsible, but you should help put the correction out$qb$, $qb$The preparer is responsible, since they produced the figure in the first place$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-020$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team missed a deadline. Your own part was finished on time. What should you say when asked?$qb$, $qb$That the team missed it, and what you will do together to prevent a repeat$qb$, $qb$That the team missed it, and explain honestly where the delay came from$qb$, $qb$That the team missed it, and that you are looking into the reasons why$qb$, $qb$That your part was done on time, and explain where the other delays were$qb$, $qb$That the delay came from another part of the team, not from your own work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-021$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realise a task you were given has been sitting undone for two weeks. Nobody has chased it. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Raise it now, say it was missed, and give a date it will be completed by$qb$, $qb$Complete it as a priority and tell the person who asked that it was delayed$qb$, $qb$Complete it quickly and mention the delay if anyone asks about the task$qb$, $qb$Complete it and say nothing, since nobody has noticed it was outstanding$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since two weeks without a chase suggests it was not needed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague takes the blame for something that was partly your doing. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell whoever is handling it that part was yours, and tell your colleague you have$qb$, $qb$Tell whoever is handling it, so your colleague is not carrying it alone$qb$, $qb$Tell your colleague you will back them up if anyone asks you about it$qb$, $qb$Thank your colleague privately and offer to help put the problem right$qb$, $qb$Say nothing, since your colleague chose to take it and may have reasons$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-023$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You promised a customer a call back by Friday. It is Friday and you still have no answer for them. What is the best action?$qb$, $qb$Call them anyway, say you have no answer yet, and give them a new date$qb$, $qb$Call them and explain what is holding the answer up and what happens next$qb$, $qb$Send a short message saying you are still working on it and will call soon$qb$, $qb$Wait until you have the answer, since calling with nothing wastes their time$qb$, $qb$Wait for them to chase, since they may not be expecting the call anyway$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-024$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are given a job you do not have the skills to do properly. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Say so now and ask for the training or support the job actually needs$qb$, $qb$Say so now and suggest who might be better placed to take it on$qb$, $qb$Take it on and ask for help with the parts you are unsure about$qb$, $qb$Take it on and learn as you go, since that is how skills are built$qb$, $qb$Take it on quietly, since refusing work may look like avoiding responsibility$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-025$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process you own has a gap that has not yet caused a problem. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Fix it now, since the absence of a problem so far is not evidence of safety$qb$, $qb$Raise it and agree when it will be fixed, so it does not get forgotten$qb$, $qb$Record it as a known risk so others are aware of it as well$qb$, $qb$Watch it for a while to see whether it causes problems in practice$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since resources are better spent on faults already causing harm$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-026$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager praises you for work that a colleague largely did. Others are present. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Say plainly in the moment that the work was largely your colleague's$qb$, $qb$Name your colleague's contribution and describe what part was yours$qb$, $qb$Thank your manager and mention your colleague's role straight afterwards$qb$, $qb$Accept the praise and tell your colleague privately that you appreciated their work$qb$, $qb$Accept the praise, since correcting a manager publicly could embarrass them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-027$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You discover that a mistake you made three months ago is still affecting a customer's account. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Report it with what happened, what it has cost the customer, and how to fix it$qb$, $qb$Report it to your manager immediately and ask how the customer should be told$qb$, $qb$Work out how much it has affected the customer, then report it with that detail$qb$, $qb$Fix the account quietly, since raising an old error now would help nobody$qb$, $qb$Fix it going forward and leave the past three months alone, as they cannot be undone$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-028$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are asked to commit to a delivery date that depends on another team you cannot control. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Commit to your part and state plainly what the other team's part depends on$qb$, $qb$Explain which parts you can guarantee and which carry risk from elsewhere$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team for their commitment before you give your own$qb$, $qb$Give the date and manage the other team closely to make sure it holds$qb$, $qb$Give the date, since raising doubts would suggest you lack confidence$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-029$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A safety check on your round was missed. You realise this at the end of your shift. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Report it before you leave, so the next shift knows the check was not done$qb$, $qb$Report it to your supervisor before leaving and offer to complete it now$qb$, $qb$Complete the check now yourself, even though your shift has ended$qb$, $qb$Note it and complete the check first thing at the start of your next shift$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since one missed check is unlikely to cause any real problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-030$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer complains about something that was genuinely not your fault. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Take responsibility for putting it right, without arguing about whose fault it was$qb$, $qb$Apologise for their experience and explain clearly what you can do about it$qb$, $qb$Explain what happened, then set out what you are able to do to help$qb$, $qb$Explain that the fault was elsewhere, then offer to point them in the right direction$qb$, $qb$Explain that it was not your fault, so they raise it with the right team$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-031$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are part of a group decision you disagreed with. It has now gone wrong. What is your position?$qb$, $qb$You own the outcome with the group, and say so without raising your objection$qb$, $qb$You own the outcome with the group, and help put the situation right$qb$, $qb$You should help fix it, whatever your view of the decision was at the time$qb$, $qb$You own it, but make sure people know you argued against it at the time$qb$, $qb$The people who supported the decision should answer for how it turned out$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-032$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You find an error in work a colleague passed to you. It is small and you can fix it in a minute. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Fix it and tell them, so they can stop the same error happening again$qb$, $qb$Fix it and mention it in passing the next time you speak to them$qb$, $qb$Fix it and let it go, since the time to raise it exceeds the time to fix it$qb$, $qb$Send it back for them to fix, since it is their work and their responsibility$qb$, $qb$Fix it silently, since raising small errors makes working relationships harder$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-033$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team is asked who was responsible for a failure. You believe it was mainly you. What should you say?$qb$, $qb$Say it was mainly you, describe what you got wrong, and what you will change$qb$, $qb$Say it was mainly you, and set out what happened from your side$qb$, $qb$Say you were closely involved and take the group through the sequence of events$qb$, $qb$Say the failure had several causes, one of which was your own part in it$qb$, $qb$Wait to see what others say, since your view of it may not be complete$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-MCQ-034$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have been covering a colleague's responsibilities and something has gone wrong in their area. How should this be handled?$qb$, $qb$You answer for the period you covered, and set out what you found and when$qb$, $qb$You answer for the period you covered, and leave the rest to your colleague$qb$, $qb$You and your colleague work out together what went wrong and when it started$qb$, $qb$Your colleague answers for it, since it is their area of responsibility$qb$, $qb$Nobody is really responsible, since the handover was temporary and unclear$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-035$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You work in a warehouse. You dropped a box of stock yesterday and put the damaged items back on the shelf, meaning to report it. You forgot. Today you see a colleague picking one of those items for a customer order. How do you handle this?$qb$, $qb$Stop him now, explain that you damaged the stock yesterday and did not report it$qb$, $qb$Stop him and tell your colleague the stock is damaged, then report it properly$qb$, $qb$Stop him, remove the damaged items, and log the damage in the system$qb$, $qb$Quietly remove the damaged items later, since the order can be picked from elsewhere$qb$, $qb$Say nothing, since the damage was minor and the customer may not even notice$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-036$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are a shift supervisor. A member of your team made an error that cost the company money. Your manager asks you what happened. How do you answer?$qb$, $qb$Explain the error, and say what your own supervision missed that allowed it$qb$, $qb$Explain the error and what you are putting in place so it does not recur$qb$, $qb$Explain what happened factually, without naming who made the error$qb$, $qb$Explain that the team member made the error and that you have spoken to them$qb$, $qb$Explain that the team member was at fault and that they have been dealt with$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-037$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have been given a project that is failing. It was already failing when you took it on. Your manager asks for an update. How do you present it?$qb$, $qb$Say where it stands, what you will do, and what you need in order to do it$qb$, $qb$Say where it stands and what you plan to do about it from this point$qb$, $qb$Say where it stands and explain the position you inherited when you took over$qb$, $qb$Say where it stands and make clear the problems began before your involvement$qb$, $qb$Say the project was already failing, so the current position was not avoidable$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-038$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer at your shop says an item you sold them last month has broken. It is outside the returns period. You remember telling them it would last for years. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge what you told them, and take the problem to your manager on that basis$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge what you said and explain what you will try to do to help them$qb$, $qb$Explain the returns policy but say you will ask your manager about an exception$qb$, $qb$Explain that the returns period has passed and suggest they contact the manufacturer$qb$, $qb$Explain the policy and say that any promise about lifespan was only a general view$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-039$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team agreed a change at a meeting you attended. You have not applied it, and now a problem has arisen because of that. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Say you did not apply the change, and say what you are doing to put it right$qb$, $qb$Say the change was not applied on your side and set out how you will correct it$qb$, $qb$Say the change was missed and ask how the team wants the problem handled$qb$, $qb$Point out that the change was not clearly communicated after the meeting$qb$, $qb$Point out that others may not have applied it either, so the fault is shared$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You gave a colleague advice that turned out to be wrong. They followed it and are now in difficulty. They have not blamed you. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Tell them the advice was wrong, and help sort out the position they are now in$qb$, $qb$Tell them the advice was mine and offer to explain that to anyone who asks$qb$, $qb$Offer to help them fix the situation, and mention that your advice was mistaken$qb$, $qb$Help them fix it, without raising whose advice led to the problem$qb$, $qb$Let it be, since they made the final decision about whether to follow the advice$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-041$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to sign off work you have not had time to check properly. The deadline is in an hour. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Say what you have and have not checked, and sign off only for the part you did$qb$, $qb$Say you cannot sign it off yet and ask for the time needed to check it$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else who knows the work to check the parts you could not$qb$, $qb$Sign it off and check it afterwards, raising anything you find at that point$qb$, $qb$Sign it off, since the deadline matters and the work is probably fine$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-042$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realise partway through the month that you have been recording your hours incorrectly, in your own favour. Nobody has queried it. How do you act?$qb$, $qb$Report it now with the correct figures, and ask for the record to be adjusted$qb$, $qb$Report it to your manager and explain how the mistake came about$qb$, $qb$Correct the record yourself from this point and mention it at your next review$qb$, $qb$Correct it going forward, since the earlier weeks were an honest mistake$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since the amount is small and correcting it would look worse$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-043$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client asks you why a piece of work is late. The real reason is that you deprioritised it to handle something else. How do you answer?$qb$, $qb$Tell them you gave it lower priority, apologise, and give a firm new date$qb$, $qb$Tell them honestly why it slipped and what date they can now rely on$qb$, $qb$Tell them it was delayed by competing work and give them a new date$qb$, $qb$Tell them it was held up by other commitments beyond your control$qb$, $qb$Tell them the delay came from the volume of work coming into the team$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are leaving a job in two weeks. You know a problem in your area will surface after you go. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Document it fully and brief whoever takes over, so it does not land on them blind$qb$, $qb$Raise it now with your manager and explain what will need attention after you leave$qb$, $qb$Write it up in your handover notes so the information is available to the next person$qb$, $qb$Mention it in passing during handover, since it will be dealt with in time$qb$, $qb$Leave it, since it will be someone else's responsibility once you have gone$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-045$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You promised to help a colleague with a task and then forgot. They completed it alone, staying late to do so. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Apologise directly, acknowledge what it cost them, and make the time up properly$qb$, $qb$Apologise and explain that you forgot, then offer to take on the next one$qb$, $qb$Apologise and thank them for covering it, then move on with the work$qb$, $qb$Explain that you were caught up in something urgent and could not get to it$qb$, $qb$Say nothing, since they managed it and raising it now would only be awkward$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-046$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager gives you feedback about a mistake. You believe the mistake was caused by unclear instructions. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Accept what went wrong, then raise the instructions as something to fix for next time$qb$, $qb$Accept the outcome and explain where the instructions left you uncertain$qb$, $qb$Ask whether the instructions could be clearer, since that contributed to it$qb$, $qb$Explain that the instructions were unclear, which is why the mistake happened$qb$, $qb$Explain that anyone would have made the same mistake with those instructions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-047$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A junior colleague you supervise has made a serious error. You did not check their work, though you were meant to. How do you handle the review?$qb$, $qb$Say the check was yours to do and was not done, alongside what they got wrong$qb$, $qb$Say that the supervision failed as well as the work, and what you will change$qb$, $qb$Describe both the error and the missing check, without weighting either one$qb$, $qb$Describe the error, and mention that supervision could have caught it earlier$qb$, $qb$Describe the error and let the review draw its own conclusions about supervision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-048$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You committed to a piece of work at a level of quality you now realise you cannot reach in the time. How do you handle it?$qb$, $qb$Say so now, and offer the choice between more time or a reduced scope$qb$, $qb$Say so now and explain what could be delivered well within the time left$qb$, $qb$Say so now and ask what matters most, so the remaining time goes there$qb$, $qb$Do your best in the time and flag the shortfall when you hand it over$qb$, $qb$Deliver what you can and explain afterwards that the timescale was too tight$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$HEAIL-ACC-SJT-049$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier makes an error that causes a problem for your customer. Your customer is asking you about it. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Own the outcome with your customer, then deal with the supplier separately$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer what happened and what you are doing to put it right$qb$, $qb$Explain that a supplier error caused it, and set out how you will resolve it$qb$, $qb$Explain that the fault sits with the supplier and that you are chasing them$qb$, $qb$Give the customer the supplier's details so they can take it up directly$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
